**3. SERVICE LEVELS AND PERFORMANCE STANDARDS**

3.1 The Contractor shall achieve the Service Levels set out in Schedule 2 throughout the term of this Agreement. The Service Levels include, without limitation:

Contract No. NGP/AEG/2020/117 specifies a duration months of 24. Record status: current.

(a) an average Plant Availability of not less than 97% in each Service Year;

(b) a monthly average Plant Availability of not less than 95%, provided that any shortfall in a single month may be recovered within the same Service Year;

(c) a response time to a Site Alarm of not more than 15 minutes during Normal Working Hours and not more than 30 minutes outside Normal Working Hours;

(d) a response time to a Critical Fault of not more than 2 hours during Normal Working Hours and not more than 4 hours outside Normal Working Hours, in each case measured from the time the Contractor receives notice of the fault;

(e) completion of Routine Maintenance within the intervals specified in the Manufacturer's Specifications, and in any event within 7 days of the scheduled date;

(f) submission of all reports required under Clause 6 by the deadlines stated therein; and

(g) compliance with all applicable health, safety, and environmental requirements, with no Lost Time Injury (LTI) attributable to the Contractor's personnel or activities.

3.2 The Contractor shall use all reasonable endeavours to achieve a Plant Availability higher than the minimum set out in Clause 3.1(a), and shall report to the Employer on the measures taken to improve availability and reduce downtime.

3.3 Availability shall be calculated in accordance with the formula set out in Schedule 2, using data from the Plant's SCADA system and the Contractor's maintenance logs. For the avoidance of doubt, time lost due to Force Majeure, grid unavailability, curtailment by the system operator, or any other cause attributable to the Employer or to a third party for which the Contractor is not responsible, shall be excluded from the calculation of Plant Availability.

3.4 If the Contractor fails to achieve any Service Level in a Service Year, the Employer's sole remedy shall be the deduction of Service Credits from the Monthly Service Fee, as set out in Schedule 3, unless such failure results from the Contractor's fraud, wilful default, or gross negligence.

(b) routine inspections of the medium-voltage collection network, step-up transformers, and the substation equipment forming part of the Plant;

(c) maintenance of all civil works, including access roads, crane pads, foundations, and drainage systems;

(d) management and operation of the SCADA system, including alarm handling, data logging, and reporting;

(e) housekeeping and general upkeep of the site, including vegetation control within the turbine hardstands.

4.2 All maintenance work shall be carried out in accordance with the Manufacturer's Specifications and the maintenance plan set out in Schedule 4, which shall be reviewed annually by the Parties. The Contractor may propose amendments to the maintenance plan, but no amendment shall take effect without the Employer's prior written approval, such approval not to be unreasonably withheld or delayed.

4.4 For the avoidance of doubt, the Contractor's maintenance obligations under this Agreement do not include:

(a) major component replacement arising from latent defects covered by the manufacturer's warranty, except where the Contractor has caused the defect through its own negligence or breach of this Agreement;

(b) repair or replacement of the high-voltage transmission line beyond the point of interconnection;

(c) capital improvements or upgrades unless expressly requested in writing by the Employer and agreed in a Variation.

**5. SAFETY AND ENVIRONMENTAL COMPLIANCE**

5.2 The Contractor shall ensure that all of its personnel assigned to the Plant hold valid and appropriate certifications, including but not limited to:

Barka Wind Farm comprises a turbine count of 36 units. Record status: current.

(a) GWO Basic Safety Training (BST) certificates for all personnel working at height or in confined spaces;

(b) valid first-aid and emergency response training;

(c) site-specific induction training, which shall be completed before any personnel are permitted to work on site.

5.3 The Contractor shall conduct a toolbox talk at the commencement of each workday and before any non-routine task. Records of toolbox talks shall be maintained and made available to the Employer upon request.

5.4 In the event of any accident, incident, or near-miss on site, the Contractor shall:

(a) take immediate action to protect persons and the environment;

(b) notify the Employer's Site Representative by telephone within 2 hours of the incident; and

(c) submit a written incident report within 24 hours, followed by a full investigation report within 7 days.

5.5 The Contractor shall comply with all environmental permit conditions and shall not cause or permit any pollution or environmental damage at the Plant. Any spillage or release of hazardous substances shall be contained and remediated in accordance with the HSE Plan, and the relevant authorities shall be notified as required by law.

5.6 The Contractor shall ensure that all waste, including used lubricants, filters, and worn components, is stored, handled, and disposed of in accordance with applicable law and good industry practice. The Contractor shall maintain a waste log and provide copies to the Employer quarterly.

